vendorPanelDetails.background:
The research, analysis and assessment should independently examine ecotourism opportunities across protected areas and other locations, moving beyond policy ambition to establish a practical, market-informed understanding of Queensland’s highest-value ecotourism prospects. The work should be grounded in a comprehensive understanding of:

• current and emerging visitor demand, both domestic and international;

• Queensland's ecotourism advantages, unique natural assets and World Heritage Areas;

• leading domestic and international ecotourism development models and case studies;

• Queensland's nature conservation, land management, and Native Title frameworks; and

• other relevant local constraints and enabling policy conditions.

Case studies should examine ecotourism projects that offer comparative learnings for Queensland, including how these projects have:

• responded and fulfilled evolving visitor demand and market trends;

• strengthened the destination’s ecotourism profile and positioning;

• successfully integrated conservation outcomes with high-quality visitor experiences;

• attracted investment from public and private sectors;

• been enabled, incentivised, developed, and scaled over time.

The final output should identify where Queensland is best placed to compete and succeed in ecotourism, the visitor markets and ecotourism experiences that present the greatest opportunities, and the enabling conditions necessary to support 45 x 45 ecotourism development across the state. The work should provide practical, evidence-based insights to guide future policy, planning and investment decisions, while ensuring ecotourism growth aligns with conservation objectives and community expectations.

